WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.160
So in last week's video I said,

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.560
"You'll never guess where I'm going next."

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.020
And your lovely wife commented

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.500
"It was a toss up between whether you were going to the dentist or the airport, honestly."

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.760
Well, the joke's on you Katherine,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.440
because I'm at the dentist.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.040
I've been to the dentist a lot

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.040
more than 150 times, actually,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.380
in the 13 long years since I was standing on a curb in Chicago reading a novel and

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.040
a bike messenger's shoulder ran into my face.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.480
The original fractures were initially misdiagnosed which led to a long-term infection and many, many surgeries

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.280
and quite a bit of ongoing pain.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.280
Alright I think I've had enough of watching that. Hi!

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.640
Greetings from the future where I'm in my basement.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.980
So Hank, this pain goes way back.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.240
In fact I complained about it in January of 2007 in the third video I ever made.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.040
"Hank, as you know, I had surgery on my mouth a few weeks ago."

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.360
I complained about it again in a video a few months ago when my mouth was still numb.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.480
And, in between, I've found little ways of talking about it without talking about it.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.580
Like, back in March of 2007, I complained

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.380
about a "doctor's visit" that was actually a dentist's visit

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.320
at which my oral surgeon told me, and I'm quoting him directly here,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.760
"You probably have nothing to worry about."

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.080
I probably have nothing to worry about,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.040
If I probably have nothing to worry about, then I definitely have something to worry about.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.980
Ah, how right you were me from the past.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.000
I should say, that person is no longer my oral surgeon

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.320
and these days I have some of the best dentists and oral surgeons around.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.300
I'm very, very lucky to have such incredible care,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.180
but, it often still hurts a lot.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.640
One of the problems with physical pain,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.460
I mean aside from the pain itself, which for me at least is literally maddening,

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.660
is that physical pain is essentially unshareable.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.760
Like, a few days ago my son was bitten by an ant

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.000
and after he told me what happened,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.640
he said "it hurt like this" and then he pinched me.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.340
He was trying to shrink the space between his pain and my understanding of it,

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:37.880
and language just wouldn't suffice.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.640
Not because he's six but because language is always inadequate in the face of pain.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.620
But, of course, being pinched doesn't really help you understand the pain of someone else's ant bite either.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.020
It's very, very hard, if not impossible to bridge that empathy gap.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.080
I've talked before about Elaine Scarry's brilliant book, The Body in Pain,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.000
which was first recommended to me by Mike Rugnetta.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.080
In one famous passage of the book, Scarry writes,

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.960
"To have great pain is to have certainty.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.720
To hear that another person has pain is to have doubt."

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.420
In that respect, and many others, physical pain can be profoundly isolating.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.500
No matter how many similes and metaphors you employ to describe the pain,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.900
No matter how many times you pinch the people you love to try to get them to understand,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.620
No one can ever know quite what it's like to have your pain,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.740
anymore than you can know what it's like to have someone else's.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.820
Pain is a reminder that you are alone inside your body,

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.700
that no one else can access your consciousness,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.900
and that what you call yourself is, at least in some ways, a kind of prison from which there is no earthly escape.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.900
a kind of prison from which there is no earthly escape.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.700
And that's like horror movie level terrifying if you think about it long enough.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.560
And that's how I feel at least when pain is turning me inward and inward in an ever tightening spiral.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.940
But that's not all pain can do.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.040
This is the part of the video where I generally take a turn towards hopefulness, Hank,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.760
which is challenging when it comes to chronic pain,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.220
but I don't think impossible.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.600
I guess that's easy for me to say because, again, I have excellent care and I don't live in constant pain.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.180
And I wanna make clear, I don't subscribe to the idea that suffering is somehow en-nobling. It sucks.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.060
But I do think when you find yourself able to turn outward,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.640
knowing pain can help you be much more empathetic.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.360
Which in turn helps us to alleviate each other's suffering,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:14.800
because sometimes listening generously to people in pain and believing them can be very good medicine.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.480
So, Hank, Nerdfighteria, thank you for listening.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.360
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

